-- title:
--   A polynomial-time $1/2$-approximation for Quantum Max-Cut
-- statement:
--   There exists one uniform polynomial-time quantum algorithm which, for every finite graph with nonnegative rational edge weights, prepares a state whose expected Quantum Max-Cut energy is at least $0.5$ times the maximum over all normalized quantum states. The Hamiltonian is $H_G=\sum_{i<j}w_{ij}(I-\mathrm{SWAP}_{ij})$. The output is generated by a uniform polynomial-time classical controller and prepared using a finite H/T/CNOT circuit from zero-initialized wires. The original graph occupies the first output wires, and all ancillas are retained. The guarantee is against every normalized complex comparison state and is exact in the fixed gate model. Empty graphs and zero weights are included. This is an open Lean goal; no accepted proof is asserted.
-- source:
--   Reviewed uniform quantum-circuit campaign model. Known 1/2 baseline: elementary greedy-matching corollary of Anshu, Gosset, and Morenz (2020), Lemma 1, Eq. (9), https://arxiv.org/html/2003.14394#S2. Independent product-state 1/2 result: Parekh and Thompson, https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08342

import Definitions.Def_QuantumMaxCutApproximationModel

theorem QuantumMaxCut.exists_half_approximation :
    ∃ A : QuantumMaxCut.Algorithm,
      QuantumMaxCut.PolynomialTime A ∧
      QuantumMaxCut.ApproximationRatio A (0.5 : ℝ) := by sorry
